Shareholder value depends on risk-adjusted future cash, subject to law and stakeholder obligations.|A project adds ₹10 NPV despite lowering current EPS.|Lower current EPS necessarily means lower value?|No|Yes|Only if cash-funded
Stakeholder trade-offs involve employees, lenders, customers, and communities; agency costs arise from divergent incentives.|Cost cuts raise short-term profit but damage service and retention.|Evaluate…?|Long-term cash and stakeholder consequences|Only current EPS|Only the cost saving
Investment selects uses, financing supplies funds, and payout distributes surplus.|A firm funds a factory with debt while maintaining cash for obligations.|A payout decision should consider…?|Investment needs and funding resilience|Only last year's dividend|Only share price
Capital budgeting evaluates long-lived investments using incremental cash and risk.|Pay ₹100 now for ₹115 next year at 10%: NPV ₹4.55.|Positive NPV suggests…?|Value creation under the assumptions|Guaranteed success|No implementation risk
Incremental cash compares with-project and without-project outcomes.|A new product earns ₹30 but reduces existing product contribution by ₹10.|Before other effects, incremental contribution is…?|₹20|₹30|₹40
Sunk costs are already incurred; opportunity costs are foregone alternatives; externalities affect other activities.|Land owned already could be sold for ₹50 if not used.|The project should consider…?|₹50 opportunity cost|Zero because owned|Only historic purchase price
Replacement changes existing operations; expansion adds capacity; mutually exclusive projects cannot all be selected.|Two machines serve the same required task with different lives.|Choosing solely by initial price is…?|Insufficient|Always optimal|Equivalent to NPV
NPV measures value; IRR rate; MIRR specified reinvestment; payback timing; profitability index value per outlay.|A small high-IRR project can create less value than a larger lower-IRR alternative.|For unconstrained exclusive choices, prioritise…?|NPV at suitable rates|Highest IRR always|Shortest payback always
Equivalent annual cost spreads present cost over comparable annual service.|Cost PV ₹100 over two years at 10% gives annual equivalent ₹57.62.|Use EAC when alternatives provide…?|Comparable repeatable service|Different unmeasured benefits|Only equal purchase prices
Capital rationing allocates a limited budget among projects.|A ₹100 budget cannot fund every positive-NPV proposal.|The aim is…?|Maximise feasible combined NPV|Pick highest price|Reject all projects
Project risk affects cash outcomes and discount-rate choice; scenarios expose joint changes.|A recession lowers volume and raises funding costs together.|This is…?|A coherent scenario|A single-cell sensitivity|A certain forecast
Real options value managerial flexibility to delay, expand, abandon, or switch.|A pilot allows expansion only after demand is observed.|Flexibility may…?|Add value relative to irrevocable commitment|Remove all risk|Guarantee expansion
Dividends and buybacks return capital; choice depends on price, taxes, liquidity, and obligations.|Buying shares above intrinsic value can harm continuing owners.|Any buyback automatically creates value?|No|Yes|Only if EPS rises
Organic growth builds capabilities; acquisitions buy them with integration and pricing risks.|Buying a rival accelerates entry but costs more than its stand-alone value.|Evaluate…?|Synergies net of premium and integration costs|Only speed|Only target revenue
Allocation performance compares returns with capital costs; incentives should reward sustainable value.|ROIC 8% on new investment against 12% cost destroys expected value.|Revenue-only bonuses may encourage…?|Unprofitable expansion|Optimal capital allocation always|Lower risk automatically
